WEBVTT

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:02.700
SAMANTHA SONG: Hi,
I'm Samantha Song

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.250
and I'm a product manager
on the Windows platform.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.270
Check this out.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.510
My keyboard looks like a
koi fish pond right now,

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:15.180
87 individual LEDs, each one a
pixel in a little aquatic scene.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.790
Fish are swimming, lily
pads are floating,

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.830
and there's ripple physics.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.020
My PC orchestrated all of this

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.900
through natural
language instructions.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.500
As developers, we spend so
much time on our computers.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.310
Half of my time awake is spent
looking at my Windows device.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.310
It should at least look and
feel personal, and not just

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.910
like a default install that
goes to a billion other people.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.430
Today, personalization
means digging

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.320
through nested settings panels

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.470
and toggling options you
didn't know existed.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.140
Want to coordinate your
accent color, wallpaper,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.520
and keyboard lighting?

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.850
That's three different
settings pages at a minimum.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.410
And if you want to go
deeper, you're looking

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.170
at potentially multiple
third-party apps

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.960
and navigating registry keys.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.600
As a developer, you
know these APIs exist,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.400
but stitching them
together into something

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.530
that feels good could
be a weekend project

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.030
that you never get to.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.000
Like here, this is just part of
the code that you would need

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.870
to write to just turn
your keyboard red.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.240
But what if you could tell your
agent that you want to turn

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.210
on your Zen coding vibe
and it handles all of it?

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.940
Your wallpaper, accent
color, dark mode,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.980
RGB lighting in one shot?

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.760
That would be fun.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.090
Or have it dynamically adjust
to your computer use needs.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.700
Like I love to watch music
videos while I work,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.460
so I customized my keyboard so
that it reflects the colors

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.220
on my screen as I have
music videos playing

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.020
in the background.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.430
So how can you do this?

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.930
Let me break it down.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:43.860
I've created a skill

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.860
that streamlines
personalization with agents.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.630
Windows provides
the capabilities

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.840
so that agents can interact
directly with the OS.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.980
Agents can generate their own
code, use MCP servers, skills,

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.080
all to accomplish the
task you have described

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.060
through natural language.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.000
Today I'll be showing you my
skill, which exists as a repo

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.430
with a skill.md at the root.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.310
That markdown file is your
contract with the agent.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.790
It declares what tools are
available, what commands to run,

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.370
and what parameters they accept.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.130
The agent reads it, reasons
about user's intent,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.900
and calls your tooling.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.860
No SDK to integrate, no
API service to design.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.530
Skills are great because
they define clear guidelines

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:26.610
for agents.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.610
So instead of spinning their
wheels, wasting tokens,

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.620
trying to interpret your intent,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.810
they know to use these
predefined tools

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.880
to accomplish a type of task.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.170
This intelligence layer,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.750
paired with exposed
Windows capabilities,

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.360
means that we can
get creative here.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.960
You say, make everything cherry
blossom themed for spring,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.670
and the agent picks
your wallpaper,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.010
chooses a pink accent color,

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.740
enables taskbar color
using skill commands

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.930
that map to real Windows APIs.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.530
The skill I'll be demoing is
an open-source Copilot skill.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.630
I am a huge fan of
colorful lights.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.140
I have my RGB dynamic
lighting enabled keyboard

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.000
and a lamp that I love.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.550
Previously, I would have to
go into settings and play

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.580
around with the preset effects
and colors to get close

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.210
to the vibe that I wanted to
feel that day, which was fine.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.220
It would just take
time, and I was limited

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.680
to what was already
available in settings,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.420
which are great effects
meant to appeal

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.400
to a broad range of users.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.910
But what if I wanted my RGB
keyboard to reflect my creative

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.620
and artistic personality?

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.740
What if I wanted it to
reflect my love of music?

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.240
Or what if I just
woke up one day

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.810
and decided I wanted my keyboard
to feel like a Zen garden

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.000
with blooming flowers?

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.940
Agents can use public
LampArray APIs to design

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.330
and implement cool per lamp
effects and animations.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.910
And that's what I've
programmed my skill to do.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:52.590
The skills modules execute it
reliably against real OS APIs.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.040
The C# driver discovers
all LampArray devices.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.920
The Python effect scripts
implement render frame,

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.310
a function, from time
to per lamp colors.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:06.750
Koi fish, shooting stars, ocean
waves, falling raindrops,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.350
all just math that
translates to color.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.020
The theme module
goes beyond RGB.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.590
You say ocean theme, and the
skill orchestrates everything

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:17.940
in a single pass.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.180
It can download themed
wallpapers, write accent colors

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.930
to the Explorer accent
registry path, and more.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.670
This skill ships with a library

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:30.330
of real Microsoft
MSIX package themes.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.820
Personalization is
the visible scenario.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.880
The underlying change is
intent becoming a first-class

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.950
system input.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.920
In this experience, I
expressed a single intent.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.120
An agent interprets it

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.120
and executes it directly
against Windows.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.050
There is no manual setup across
themes, lighting, or settings.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.260
The system treats it as
one coherent action.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.920
And from there, you can start
to see the art of the possible.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.380
At the enterprise level,
you could imagine a world

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.190
where a user switches into
a secure finance mode,

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.100
and the system aligns
apps, access boundaries,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.500
and environment automatically.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.080
And for developers, you could
imagine skills becoming the

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.630
interface layer, where
instead of scripts

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.320
and fragmented tooling,
capabilities are defined once

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.560
and reused consistently
by agents.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.660
Windows is evolving
into a platform

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.960
where natural language can
map to real system outcomes,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.100
with the structure and
primitives needed to make

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.040
that reliable, safe,
and extensible.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.900
To build the skill
I'll be demoing today,

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.600
I described my vision
to GitHub Copilot,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.760
and it scaffolded
the.NET driver,

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.400
wrote the effect scripts, built
the theme engine, everything.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.250
I used a GitHub Copilot CLI,

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.440
which you can install
from the link here.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.920
And I'm also showing a link to
the public repo for my skill.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.980
First, I'm going to show off
how agents can generate custom

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.310
animations based on
a user's prompt.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.590
My keyboard is running
one right now.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.170
This effect actually
came to me in a dream.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.120
I was like, what if my
keyboard could look

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.280
like a koi fish
swimming in a pond?

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.930
I gave this prompt to my agent,

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.300
and it generated
a pond simulation

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.990
with the 87 individual
LEDs in my keyboard.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.390
You can see the swimming
fish, lily pads, ripples,

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:17.640
all from just a few
lines of Python.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.860
Let's see what other
effects it can generate.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.680
Hmm, what about a
cozy campfire theme

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.590
for all my dynamic lighting
compatible devices?

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.600
Let's see what it comes up with.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.280
Code generation on Windows.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.930
So powerful.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.430
See what happened?

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.790
The agent reasoned,
generated a Python script,

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.730
implemented render frame
with the fire effects,

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.250
and ran it on my
keyboard and lamp.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.920
That's agentic code
generation executing

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.470
against real Windows APIs.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.240
But why just stop
at my keyboard?

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.070
With Windows primitives,
I should be able

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.230
to change my whole
Windows theme.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.800
I'm talking wallpaper, accent
color, light mode, dark mode.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.130
One sentence to my agent,
and I can change everything.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.740
Let's say I open my
desktop in the morning,

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.190
and I see the classic Windows
Bloom theme, which is beautiful

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.770
but maybe doesn't
really feel like me.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:09.870
Today, I'm feeling
a bit stressed.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.100
I have a ton of meetings

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.010
and several vibe coding
projects to work on.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.050
Let's start the day
with a Zen coding theme

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.000
to facilitate focus.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Let me just type that into my
CLI, and let's see what happens.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.680
So much better.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.860
The themes agent can generate
a completely new theme,

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.920
or it can pull a beautiful
Microsoft design theme,

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.750
which is what
you're seeing here.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:44.430
And check out my keyboard too.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.070
A new calming effect.

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.900
And if I wanted to
switch it up later --

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:55.110
-- cherry blossom theme.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.150
Boom, everything changes again.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.370
Okay, these effects
are super cool,

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.070
and my Windows is starting
to feel more like me.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.290
But I'm a super
multitasker who's looking

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:07.200
for productivity gains
wherever I can find them.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.190
How can my personalization agent
help me be more productive?

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.170
I don't have my sound
on when I'm focusing,

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.600
so often I miss
my notifications.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.100
So what if I could flash red
whenever I get a notification?

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.330
Let me just tell my agent

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.880
to flash my keyboard red
whenever I get a message

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.770
so that I can stay
on top of things.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.830
The notification watcher
monitors Windows toast events

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.320
and resumes with no state loss.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.830
Now, even if I'm in deep focus
mode, if Monica messages me,

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.890
I'll be able to see it, respond,
and go back to focusing

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.450
without missing a beat.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.480
Theming and personalization
is so important to Windows.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.450
We want developers to have fun
coding on Windows and to feel

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:47.250
like their machine is theirs.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.650
We are investing in
exposing Windows primitive

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.120
so that agents can do more

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:53.670
with the platform and
make it personal.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.140
Okay, so to finish up here,

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:56.970
there are three things
I want you to do.

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:59.750
First, clone the skill
and make it yours,

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:03.800
github.com/samanthamsong/windows
personalization skill.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.940
Run the setup and
you're up and running.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.760
Use the effects, themes,
and Spotify sync

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.760
that are already there,
or describe something new

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.430
to your agent and
watch it generate it.

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:17.370
Second, build your own
skill and share it.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.890
The skill.md contract is simple.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.870
Declare your tools,
write some scripts,

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:24.240
and any agent can invoke them.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:27.570
Got an idea for a focus mode
skill, a weather-reactive theme

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:31.650
that matches the sky outside, a
meeting-aware theme switcher,

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.210
and the final call to action,

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.480
let's crowdsource Windows
personalization together.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:37.590
The repo is open.

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.440
I want this to be a place where
developers share their skills,

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:43.670
their effects, and their
weird and wonderful ideas.

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:47.030
Your koi fish might inspire
someone else's jellyfish.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:50.690
Your synth wave theme might
become someone's daily driver.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.540
Fork my skill, build
something cool, submit a PR,

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.940
and help make Windows
PC feel like it belongs

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:58.200
to the person using it.
